# Shell scripts must keep LF endings, on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default, so a clone there
# rewrites every text file to CRLF. For a .java or .jsx file that is harmless.
# For a script with a `#!` line it is not: bash reads the carriage return as
# part of the interpreter path and of every command, and the failure is
#
#     bash: aws/deploy.sh: /usr/bin/env^M: bad interpreter
#     $'\r': command not found
#
# which names a character nobody typed and points at nothing that is wrong.
# Someone deploying this from Git Bash on Windows hits it on their first
# command, and there is no clue in it that the cause is a checkout setting.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by the Amazon Linux instance and by psql, not
# by anything on the machine that cloned them.
*.sql text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf

# Let Git decide for everything else, and never touch binaries.
* text=auto
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pptx binary
*.onnx binary
*.geojson text eol=lf
